              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contradiction"} contradiction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            into the empty type are regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Like all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation"} type constructors
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} construct elements
            of it, how to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination"} use such elements
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rules"} computation rules
          %p
            To start with, since the empty type is not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation"} type formation
            rule just says that it exists:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{ }{\emptyset\colon Type}
          %h3#as_a_positive_type As a positive type
          %p
            The empty type is most naturally presented as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+type"} positive type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} constructor rules
            are primary. However, since the empty type is supposed to contain no elements, there
            %em are
            no constructor rules.
          %p
            In fact one may understand the positively defined empty type as that
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            which is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %em no constructors
          %p
            Therefore, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
            supporting a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+constructions"} calculus of constructions
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+types"} data types
            using literally an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+%28computer+science%29"} string
            of constructors on the right:
          %pre
            %code Inductive empty : Type :=
          %h4#RecursionPrinciple Recursion principle
          %p
            We start by considering the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion+principle"} recursion principle
            of the empty type, hence its un-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+rule"} elimination rule
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination"} eliminator rules
            are derived from the constructor rules in the usual way: to use a term
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \,\colon\, \varnothing
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \,\colon\, Type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \,\colon\, \varnothing
            could have been constructed. But for the empty type there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} no such
            ways and hence –
            %em assuming
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \,\colon\, \varnothing
            – we obtain a term of any type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            under no further conditions:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∅
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi ind
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{
                  D \,\colon\, Type
                  }
                  {
                  x \,\colon\, \varnothing
                  \\;\; \vdash \;\;
                  ind_D(x) \,\colon\, D
                  }
          %p Some ways to read this recursion principle:
          %ul
            %li
              Understood as a statement in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
              (regarding
              = succeed ")," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
              assumption every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implied
              (
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ex+falso+quodlibet"} ex falso quodlibet
              ):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi False
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi D
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  False \;\Rightarrow\; D
                  \\,.
          %ul
            %li
              Understood in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
              (cf. the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              into any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∅
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∃
                  %mi D
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\varnothing \xrightarrow{\exists} D
                  \\,.
          %p
            What is not manifest from the above rule is the (essential) uniqueness of these morphisms; on that point see also the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EtaConversion"} eta conversion rule
          %h4#InductionPrinciple Induction principle
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            the elimination rule of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            is a dependent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction+principle"} induction principle
            which involves any
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
          %p#InferenceRules
            Applying the general rules for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+types"} inductive types
            to the case of no generators, one obtains the following
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation+rule"}> type formation rule
              \:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mpadded{:lspace => "-50%width", :width => "0"}
                        %mphantom
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ∅
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{
                  }{
                  \\mathclap{\phantom{\vert^{\vert}}}
                  \\varnothing \,\colon\, Type
                  }
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction+rule"}> term introduction rule
              \:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext --- none ---
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\text{--- none ---}
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination+rule"}> term elimination rule
              \:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∅
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mpadded{:lspace => "-50%width", :width => "0"}
                        %mphantom
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi ind
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mo ∅
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{
                  x \,\colon\, \varnothing
                  \\;\vdash\;\;
                  D(x) \,:\, Type
                  }{
                  \\mathclap{\phantom{\vert^{\vert}}}
                  ind_{(D)}
                  \\,\colon\,
                  \\underset{x \colon \varnothing}{\prod}
                  D(x)
                  }
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rule"}> computation rule
              \:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext --- none ---
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\text{--- none ---}
          %p
            Notice that the induction principle entails the recursion principle
            %a{:href => "#RecursionPrinciple"} above
            (cf. e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#MHE"} MHE, §2.6
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi Type
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ∅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo ∅
                        %mo →
                        %mi Type
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ind
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ↦
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo ∅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{
                  \\frac{
                  D \,\colon\, Type
                  }
                  {
                  \\big(
                  (x \,\colon\, \varnothing)
                  \\mapsto
                  D
                  \\big)
                  \\,\colon\,
                  (\varnothing \to Type)
                  }
                  }{
                  ind_{ (x \mapsto D) }
                  \\,\colon\,
                  (x \colon \varnothing) \to D
                  }
          %h4#EtaConversion Eta-conversion
          %p
            Notice that there is no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-reduction"} beta-reduction
            rule for the positive empty type, since there are no constructors to compose with the eliminator.
          %p
            However, one may consider an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-conversion"} eta-conversion
            rule, which says that for any term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∅
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \,\colon\, \varnothing \;\;\vdash\;\; c \,\colon\, C
            in a context including a term of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi abort
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi η
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  abort_C(e)
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\leftrightarrow_\eta
                  \\;\;\;
                  c
                  \\,.
          %p
            As with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-conversion"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              \-conversion
            rule for the negative presentation of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            %em reduction
            (since we cannot determine
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            from
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi abort
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} abort_C(e)
            = succeed "." do
              %em expansion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            implements the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+reduction"} beta reduction
            rule, but not the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta+conversion"} eta conversion
            (although eta equivalence is provable for the inductively defined
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
          %h3#as_a_negative_type As a negative type
          %p
            As for binary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+types"} sum types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+type"} negative type
            as well, but only if we allow
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequents"} sequents
            with multiple conclusions. This is common in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            presentations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %p
            The two definitions are provably equivalent, but only using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction rule
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            they become distinct; the positive empty type is “zero”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{0}
            and the negative one is “bottom”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
          %h3#using_a_type_of_propositions Using a type of propositions
          %p
            Suppose that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+propositions"} type of propositions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            \-
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{A:U} \mathrm{isProp}(A)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+function+type"} dependent function type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ≡
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \equiv \prod_{P:\mathrm{Prop}} P
          %p
            whose elements are witnesses that all propositions are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+types"} pointed types
            (and thus
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+types"} contractible types
          %p
            By
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+function+extensionality"} weak function extensionality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}
            has an element and is contractible.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#equivalence_of_the_definitions_of_the_empty_type Equivalence of the definitions of the empty type
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p The inductive definition of the empty type and the definition of the empty type in terms of dependent product types and the type of propositions are equivalent to each other.
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Since the empty type is a proposition, it is equivalent to its own
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo ≃
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \simeq [\emptyset]
            %p
              Meanwhile, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
              of a type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is equivalent to the dependent product type
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A] \simeq \prod_{P:\mathrm{Prop}} (A \to P) \to P
            %p
              Substituting the empty type for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\emptyset] \simeq \prod_{P:\mathrm{Prop}} (\emptyset \to P) \to P
            %p
              By the recursion principle of the empty type, for every type
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \to P
              is contractible, and for every contractible type
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to P
              is equivalent to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mo :
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mo :
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                      %mi P
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \simeq [\emptyset] \simeq \left(\prod_{P:\mathrm{Prop}} (\emptyset \to P) \to P\right) \simeq \left(\prod_{P:\mathrm{Prop}} P\right)
            %p
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %div
            %ul
              %li
                %p
